<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Sousse</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Hadrumetum</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Sousse is a city on the eastern coast of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tunisia">Tunisia</a>, founded by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/phoenicians">Phoenicians</a> as Hadrumetum and later the capital of the Roman province of Byzacena. Renamed Justinianopolis under Byzantine rule, it became an Aghlabid naval base after the Arab conquest, with a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ribat">ribat</a> and a Great Mosque built in the 9th century. Its medina is a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco-world-heritage-site">UNESCO World Heritage Site</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/sousse.jpg" alt="Sousse" width="320" height="196" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Mietek Ł · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Tunezja,_Sousse_-_panoramio_(3).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Hundreds of towns flourished, from Carthage and <b>Hadrumetum</b> to Thugga, Thuburbo Maius, Sufetula, Bulla Regia, Thysdrus and Leptis Magna, with forums, baths, theatres, amphitheatres and floor mosaics of outstanding quality.</blockquote><a href="/empire/africa-proconsularis">Africa Proconsularis · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>Ribats guarded the coast at <b>Sousse</b> and Monastir, and the Ribat of Sousse and the Ribat of Monastir still stand.</blockquote><a href="/empire/aghlabid-emirate">Aghlabid emirate · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>The southern part of the old province, the region known as Byzacium, became Byzacena, with its capital at <b>Hadrumetum</b> (Sousse), and was governed by a consularis within the Diocese of Africa.</blockquote><a href="/state/byzacena">Byzacena</a></li><li><blockquote>His admiral George of Antioch took Tripoli in 1146, and in 1148 Mahdia, <b>Sousse</b> and Sfax fell as the Zirid ruler al-Hasan fled.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-africa">Kingdom of Africa</a></li></ul></section>
